      "note": "Pokorny's lemma at p.429 is 'ghel-1 (und ghel-1), also as i-, u- or n-stem; ghelə- : ghlē-, ghlō- : ghlā-, Meaning: to shine; green, gold, blue, yellow, moon', which is Nourai's 'to shine, also a bright yellow color' with his page number, and Walde-Pokorny I 624 is the same article. Modern reconstructions write the root *ǵʰelh₃-. The whole Iranian side of the chart is Pokorny's own paragraph on p.429: 'Avestan zari- „yellow, yellowish, golden“, zairita- „yellow, pale-yellow“, zaranya-, Old pers. daraniya- n. „gold“, zaranaena „from gold, golden“, zara- m. „gall“ (= gr. χολή), named after the color as gr. χόλος, Latin fel, Old Norse gall'. That single sentence underwrites nodes #1, #14 and #15 at once. Two things need correcting. (1) Node #8: Greek arsenikón cannot come from Arabic az-zarnīḵ — it is attested from the 4th c. BCE, a thousand years before Arabic; Klein (KLN:108, Nourai's own reference) says the Greek came from Middle Persian *zarnīk 'through the medium of a Semitic language', i.e. Syriac zarnīḵā. Nourai is following Pihan's 1866 dictionary, which really does call az-zernīkh the 'prototype de la transcription grecque'. (2) Node #15 labels 'zahr, zara 1' Avestan/Old Persian: only zāra- is Avestan; zahr is Middle Persian and, as a separate word meaning 'poison', Nyberg derives it from *jaθra- 'means of killing'. The 'gall' word is MP zahrag > NP زهره. Everything else on the page — zar, zard, zardak, zargūn, zyrn, daraniya, daraniya-kara, zahre, zircon, zirconium — checks out.",
