          "derivation": "French jargon (also jargoon) 'a yellowish gem, a variety of zircon' is indeed ultimately Persian zargūn, but no source makes the loan direct. Klein routes it 'F., fr. Port. zarcão, fr. Arab. zarqān, „minium“, ult. fr. Pers. zargūn, „gold-colored“, compound of zar, „gold“ ... and gūn, „color“'; Devic reaches the same conclusion from the Romance side, identifying jargon and zircon with Spanish azarcón / Portuguese zarcão and 'un terme arabe zarqoûn, avec l'article az-zarqoûn, qui se disait du minium'; Etymonline records the medieval Latin jargonce and Italian giargone and the same possible Arabic zarkun. So the historical route runs Persian zargūn → Arabic zarqūn 'red lead, minium' → Ibero-Romance zarcão/azarcón → Italian giargone → medieval Latin jargonce → 18th-c. French jargon, the sense drifting from 'red lead' to 'gold-coloured gem'. Nourai's arrow is right in substance but omits the Arabic and Romance stages that every source insists on, and this is the same jargon that the identical-looking word for 'gibberish' (from the gargle root) is not related to.",
